  "response": "The supplied snapshot does not show a dedicated test for an omitted `timeout` argument on `Session.request`, `Session.get`, `Session.post`, or the other session wrappers. The closest omission is `test_encoded_methods`, which calls `requests.request(...)` without `timeout`, but it only asserts that the response is successful and does not inspect timeout propagation [Data: Sources (98)].\n\nExplicit `timeout=None` is covered by `TestTimeout.test_none_timeout` in `tests/test_requests.py`, at `tests/test_requests.py:L2414-L2570`. Its parameterization passes `None` explicitly, and the test asserts that the request succeeds with status code 200. The same test also covers `Urllib3Timeout(connect=None, read=None)`, which is a distinct timeout-object case [Data: Sources (98)].\n\nA float timeout is covered by `TestTimeout.test_stream_timeout`, which calls `requests.get(httpbin(\"delay/10\"), timeout=2.0)` and checks for a read-timeout error. Two-value `(connect, read)` shapes are covered by `test_read_timeout` with `(None, 0.1)`, `test_connect_timeout` with `(0.1, None)`, and `test_total_timeout_connect` with `(0.1, 0.1)`. Each also covers the corresponding `Urllib3Timeout(connect=..., read=...)` form [Data: Sources (98)]. The snapshot therefore establishes coverage for explicit values, but is insufficient to establish coverage for a per-instance `Session.default_timeout`, distinguishing omission from explicit `None`, or preserving an effective timeout across redirects.",
  "score": 91,
